WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.840
The Walking Dead boasts one of the biggest&nbsp;
casts on TV. As a result, it's enjoyed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.840
whole lot of unique cameos -- so many, in fact,&nbsp;
that you might not have noticed most of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:18.240
These are the most surprising, interesting, and&nbsp;
just plain strange cameos on The Walking Dead.

00:00:19.354 --> 00:00:24.800
Hines Ward was among the NFL's most athletic,&nbsp;
dazzling, and accomplished wide receivers in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:31.760
late 1990s and 2000s. In his 14-season career,&nbsp;
spent entirely with the Pittsburgh Steelers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.040
he managed 1,000 successful receptions,&nbsp;
placing him high on the all-time list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.640
which led to four Pro Bowl&nbsp;
selections and two Super Bowl rings.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.520
Ward was drafted out of the University of&nbsp;
Georgia, where one of his teammates on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.720
gridiron was running back IronE Singleton,&nbsp;
who pursued acting upon graduation.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.760
Singleton found success in Hollywood, landing&nbsp;
roles in movies like Remember the Titans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.480
The Blind Side, and The Longest Yard – and&nbsp;
eventually, on the first three seasons of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:06.560
The Walking Dead, in which he portrayed zombie&nbsp;
apocalypse survivor Theodore Douglas, AKA T-Dog.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:12.960
In 2012, Ward utilized his connection to Singleton&nbsp;
to land a small role on The Walking Dead as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.600
which was then shooting in&nbsp;
his home state of Georgia.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.720
When the show's location moved&nbsp;
to a semi-abandoned prison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.000
Ward got to play one of the zombies who pounce&nbsp;
on the main survivor characters. Ward spent two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.640
hours in makeup to be transformed into a walker,&nbsp;
where an artist painted his old jersey number, 86,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:35.520
onto the back of his head. Even as a stumbling&nbsp;
zombie, we wouldn't want to go up against him.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.880
Before The Walking Dead hit AMC in&nbsp;
2010 and produced some of the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:47.760
viewership numbers in cable TV history, zombie&nbsp;
entertainment was primarily seen as niche fare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:53.680
enjoyed by only a relatively small segment of the&nbsp;
population. Sure, everyone knew what zombies were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:59.040
but only dedicated fans could offer opinions&nbsp;
on Night of the Living Dead or discuss the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:05.440
finer points of Re-Animator. Zombies were,&nbsp;
in other words, for nerds — but then those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.160
much-maligned individuals enjoyed&nbsp;
a big moment in the early 2010s.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:15.600
Comedian Chris Hardwick, an avowed nerd, had&nbsp;
just launched his popular Nerdist podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:21.840
the first brick in a media empire that caters&nbsp;
to, well, unabashed nerds like himself.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.800
When The Walking Dead returned for its&nbsp;
highly anticipated second season in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:32.080
new episodes were followed by a new series:&nbsp;
Talking Dead. On Talking Dead, cast and crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.040
from The Walking Dead and special celebrity guests&nbsp;
gather to talk about the episode that just aired.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.200
The whole endeavor is hosted by the always&nbsp;
enthusiastic and proudly nerdy Hardwick.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:42.960
"Holy shit!"

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.040
Those two shows collided, and Hardwick&nbsp;
earned himself a major thrill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:54.320
when, in 2019, the Nerdist founder made a&nbsp;
brief, hard-to-spot appearance on The Walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:00.480
Dead. Near the end of the episode "Scars," a&nbsp;
walker tries to attack Judith. That zombie is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.840
none other than Hardwick, who achieved, in&nbsp;
that moment, the peak of zombie nerd-dom.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:13.680
Anthrax's Scott Ian is one of a rare breed: the&nbsp;
most famous member of a band, even though he isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.800
the lead singer. Since the 1980s, Ian has played&nbsp;
guitar in the legendary thrash metal group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:24.720
and has perpetually overshadowed the group's&nbsp;
various singers. He sports a memorable look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:30.400
what with his shaved head and long, pointy wizard&nbsp;
beard. Ian is also a big fan of horror media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:35.360
and once hosted a web series called Bloodworks,&nbsp;
where he explored how gore effects are made.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:38.720
"It's 5:15 or something in the&nbsp;
morning here in Atlanta, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:40.240
we're on the set of The Walking Dead."

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.200
All these unique qualities, along with&nbsp;
his friendship with Walking Dead special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:50.960
effects makeup designer Greg Nicotero, helped&nbsp;
land Ian a gig on cable's top zombie show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:56.400
In 2011, Ian was rendered unrecognizable&nbsp;
by a thick slathering of gore and makeup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.080
effects in his appearance as a walker&nbsp;
on a webisode of The Walking Dead.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.520
He returned to the franchise&nbsp;
in the 2015 episode "Remember":&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.320
His anonymous zombie hides under a trash&nbsp;
heap and reaches out to grab the leg of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.760
an unsuspecting Rick Grimes, but is quickly&nbsp;
stopped when the sheriff allows his son, Carl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:20.800
to shove a metal pole through the undead Ian's&nbsp;
head, instantly dispatching him. What a gory way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:26.400
to go — but then again, we're not sure this&nbsp;
horror hound would be happy with anything less.

00:04:27.429 --> 00:04:32.240
Sam Witwer has done voice work for the Star&nbsp;
Wars universe, held the major role of Davis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:37.600
Bloome for a season of the Superman-centric&nbsp;
series Smallville, played vampire Aidan Waite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:44.480
on four seasons of Being Human, and portrayed&nbsp;
Mr. Hyde, of horror legend, on Once Upon a Time.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:50.320
Nearly a decade into his bustling career as an&nbsp;
actor in both film and TV, Witwer took a role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:55.840
on the first episode of The Walking Dead – one&nbsp;
with no lines and scant screen time. The part:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.760
"Tank Soldier," a zombified ex-military man&nbsp;
that Rick Grimes has to kill in Atlanta.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:05.520
Witwer was supposed to return to the show&nbsp;
later on, as showrunner Frank Darabont&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.920
wanted to produce some prequel episodes&nbsp;
about the fall of Atlanta to zombies.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:14.000
As originally planned, Witwer would have&nbsp;
reportedly had a more robust role playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:19.600
that soldier before his zombification, but&nbsp;
unfortunately, those episodes never came to pass.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:26.240
Most television viewers and filmgoers might&nbsp;
not know M.C. Gainey by name, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:32.640
certainly recognize his face. The prolific&nbsp;
character actor has appeared in nearly 200&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:39.680
projects, usually in small but pivotal roles, and&nbsp;
typically as a villain. Gainey plays angry guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.680
corrupt authority figures,&nbsp;
violent criminals, and the like:

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.360
His impressive resume includes roles like Big John&nbsp;
Brittle in Django Unchained, Tom Friendly on Lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:53.760
and Poppa Poutine on Riverdale. If a&nbsp;
character played by Gainey shows up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.760 --> 00:06:00.480
it generally means very bad news for the film or&nbsp;
TV series' protagonist. Gainey told The Junction,

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:04.960
"With a face like this, there aren't a lot&nbsp;
of lawyers or priest roles coming my way."

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:12.800
It's true: Gainey's mug is a craggy marvel that&nbsp;
was made to loom menacingly out of the shadows.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:17.440
Audiences are likely to be most familiar with&nbsp;
Gainey for his work as Swamp Thing in Con Air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:23.600
or perhaps Bo Crowder on Justified, but probably&nbsp;
not at all from his time on The Walking Dead.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:29.840
He appeared just once on The Walking Dead, In the&nbsp;
2014 episode "Slabtown," his recognizable face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:34.880
heavily obscured by thick makeup so he&nbsp;
could amble around as an anonymous zombie.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:39.840
In 2002, Keisha Castle-Hughes&nbsp;
enjoyed what is probably the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:45.760
best response an actor can get for their&nbsp;
debut performance. At just 12 years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:50.240
the Australian-born actress starred in the&nbsp;
highly acclaimed New Zealand-produced film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:55.280
Whale Rider, for which she earned an Academy&nbsp;
Award nomination for Best Actress. She was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.680
at the time, the youngest person to&nbsp;
ever be recognized in that category.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:05.440
She then re-emerged in 2015 as the fierce&nbsp;
Dornish warrior Obara Sand on Game of Thrones.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:08.160
Just before she joined the cast of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:13.520
megawatt HBO series, Castle-Hughes&nbsp;
booked a spot on The Walking Dead.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.520
In the 2014 episode "Slabtown," she portrayed&nbsp;
Joan, a zombie outbreak survivor held prisoner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:25.920
by Gorman. Bitten by a walker during an escape&nbsp;
attempt, she endures a grueling arm amputation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:31.360
but ultimately takes her own life by opening&nbsp;
her stitches and allowing herself to bleed out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:35.360
Joan then reanimates as a zombie,&nbsp;
and is killed by Dawn Lerner.

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:39.040
Though The Walking Dead's ratings&nbsp;
figures had fallen off from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:44.160
stratospheric levels the show once reached&nbsp;
at its peak, it still remained one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:49.600
most popular and high-profile shows on&nbsp;
cable TV by the time 2019 rolled around.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:54.560
All throughout the series' run, it's&nbsp;
been a lucrative gig for actors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:59.200
Landing a role on the show can serve&nbsp;
as marvelous exposure for any actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:04.320
even one who has already established themselves&nbsp;
on other well-known television series -- and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:10.240
even if you're just there for a one-off&nbsp;
role to show up, zombie out, and get shot.

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:13.040
That's very much the case for Rutina Wesley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:18.800
who first found fame as Tara Thornton on HBO's&nbsp;
lauded vampire saga, True Blood. After her time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:25.600
on that show ended in 2014, Wesley enjoyed a&nbsp;
long arc on NBC's Hannibal as Reba McClane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:32.560
as well as a couple of guest spots on the CW's&nbsp;
flagship DC superhero series, Arrow. In 2016, she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:38.240
began starring as Nova Bordelon on the celebrated&nbsp;
Oprah Winfrey Network family drama, Queen Sugar.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:43.840
One truth emerges from Wesley's&nbsp;
filmography: She's busy, flexible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:50.080
and just as comfortable portraying the nuances of&nbsp;
bereavement as she is hanging out with vampires.

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:54.560
Despite her packed schedule, Wesley&nbsp;
found time to appear on a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.560 --> 00:09:00.240
episode of The Walking Dead in 2019. In&nbsp;
"Scars," Wesley portrayed Jocelyn, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:05.920
Joss, a cruel and controlling leader of a&nbsp;
cult-like collective of children. Michonne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:10.960
her old friend from before the end of the world&nbsp;
arrived, is forced to end her wicked ways.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.520
In addition to The Walking Dead&nbsp;
comics series writer Robert Kirkman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:19.760
and the TV series developer Frank&nbsp;
Darabont, the one person who has had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:24.800
the biggest behind-the-scenes impact on the&nbsp;
long-running zombie show is Greg Nicotero.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:29.600
He's directed more than 30 episodes,&nbsp;
served as various types of producer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:33.920
and heads up the show's special effects&nbsp;
make-up department — which means he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:40.480
responsible for turning groups of actors into&nbsp;
monstrously flesh-dripping zombie hordes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:45.520
To say he's an integral part of the&nbsp;
show is a massive understatement.

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:50.000
Every now and then, Nicotero even gets in on&nbsp;
the fun, taking a spin in the zombification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:55.280
chair himself. Between 2010 and 2016,&nbsp;
Nicotero appeared in five episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.280 --> 00:10:00.560
each time as an undead walker. His Walking Dead&nbsp;
colleagues tend to grant him some of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:05.360
plum zombie assignments available. In one&nbsp;
appearance set in the town of Woodbury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:10.880
for example, he was tasked with lunging at Andrea,&nbsp;
who stops him with a pistol blast to the head.

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:15.760
"This is my friend. This is Greg Nicotero."

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:21.920
A TV adaptation of The Walking Dead might look&nbsp;
and feel completely different if not for the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:27.360
of Charlie Adlard. In 2004, Adlard joined the&nbsp;
creative staff of the comic series upon which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:32.640
the show is based as a penciler. From there on,&nbsp;
he helped bring the stories of Robert Kirkman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:37.680
and his other collaborators to life,&nbsp;
until its final issue hit stands in 2019.

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:41.840
When you think of The Walking Dead&nbsp;
comic, what you picture is Adlard's work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:46.160
His vision of the apocalypse, full of&nbsp;
dark, inky shadows and weathered faces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:51.280
is a terrifying triumph of comics art,&nbsp;
to say nothing of the fact that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:56.400
formed the foundation of what became&nbsp;
one of the most popular shows on TV.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.960
Appropriately enough, Adlard got to be&nbsp;
part of the TV world he helped to realize.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:05.680
During production of the show's first season in&nbsp;
the summer of 2010, he reported to the set of The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:11.120
Walking Dead to spend three days filming a cameo&nbsp;
appearance as a walker. As he later recounted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:17.360
in an interview with comicbookmovie.com, it&nbsp;
was a thrilling experience. As he recalled,

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:23.760
"Three days of seeing my creations come&nbsp;
to life ... I felt very privileged."

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:28.240
Adlard can be seen amongst the horde&nbsp;
of walkers that attacks a very confused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:33.920
Sheriff Rick Grimes in Atlanta in "Days Gone&nbsp;
By," the very first episode of The Walking Dead.

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:40.320
After building up a reputation as a versatile&nbsp;
actor and something of a muse for filmmaker Tim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:46.160
Burton in quirky classics like Ed Wood,&nbsp;
Edward Scissorhands, and Sleepy Hollow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:51.600
Johnny Depp became one of the biggest movie&nbsp;
stars in the world in the 21st century.

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:56.560
He's starred as Captain Jack Sparrow in&nbsp;
five enormously successful Pirates of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:01.680
the Caribbean movies, as the Mad Hatter in&nbsp;
two Alice in Wonderland adventures, and as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:06.960
Gellert Grindelwald in the Harry Potter prequel&nbsp;
Fantastic Beasts: The Crimes of Grindelwald.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:12.400
In 2016, Depp even made time for a brief guest&nbsp;
star appearance on an episode of The Walking Dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:18.880
... or at least part of him did, and apparently&nbsp;
without his knowledge or active participation.

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:24.400
In the episode "Not Tomorrow Yet," a decapitated&nbsp;
human head makes an appearance that bears a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:28.960
strong resemblance to Depp. According&nbsp;
to makeup and effects wizard and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:35.440
"Not Tomorrow Yet" director Greg Nicotero, that's&nbsp;
because the head is, in fact, Johnny Depp's head.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:37.920
Nicotero told Entertainment Weekly,

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:42.640
"I think we had sculpted an emaciated&nbsp;
version of a dummy head for something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:46.880
and we used Johnny Depp's head as&nbsp;
a basis just for a clay sculpt."

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:50.320
That's certainly one way of making an appearance.

